\section{Introduction and usage}
\label{tex:Intro}

The aim of this include file is to make building LaTeX documents easier.
First we want to add suffix rules for LaTeX similar to those already in 
make(1)~\cite[see][Sect.\ 10.2]{GNUMake} for \eg (plain) TeX and C.

The main goal is to improve the default rules of GNU make; to change from plain 
TeX to LaTeX, to change from DVI-format to PDF-format.
We provide several suffix rules:
first, for ordinary documents, \ie to compile a [[.tex]] file to [[.pdf]],
but we also provide suffix rules for [[.dvi]];
second, for classes and packages, \ie to compile
\begin{enumerate*}
\item a DocTeX [[.dtx]] file to [[.pdf]] or [[.dvi]] and
\item an [[.ins]] file to [[.cls]] or [[.sty]].
\end{enumerate*}
The suffix rules we provide here follows the conventions set out 
in~\cite[Sect.\ 10.2]{GNUMake}.

To do the main LaTeX compilation, we use the following set up.
We note that [[xelatex]] requires the [[-8bit]] flag to be able to handle tabs 
in [[minted]] output.
Although, the option [[-halt-on-error]] seems interesting to stop the 
compilation if an error occurs, so that we don't miss it, it doesn't work.
The reason is that [[latexmk]] doesn't stop on error, it just keeps on going.
\TeX{} on the other hand stops that round of execution, but doesn't ask for 
input---which it does otherwise.
So the [[-halt-on-error]] option actually makes it more likely that we miss 
that an error occurred.
For the same reason we leave \TeX{} in its default interactive mode rather
than passing [[-interaction=nonstopmode]]: when \TeX{} stops at an error we
want to see it, not have the run plough on and bury the error in the log.

A compilation has three possible outcomes, and make(1) must be able to tell
them apart.
\begin{description}
\item[The document compiled] There is nothing to say, and the recipe succeeds
  silently.
\item[The document is broken] The recipe fails with the status of the command
  that compiled it, and removes its target.
  A build that cannot fail cannot be observed to have failed: the documentation
  of nytid\footnote{%
    URL: \url{https://github.com/dbosk/nytid}
  } exited zero with an \enquote{Emergency stop} and a bibtex(1)
  \enquote{Error 2} in its log, which is how a clean clone that no longer built
  stayed hidden.
\item[The document did not settle] The recipe warns and succeeds, provided there
  is any output.
  Cross-references or page breaks may still be off.
  Some documents have no fixed point at all, such as one with a noweb
  [[longxref]] continuation list, whose line count feeds back into the
  pagination it describes.
  No number of passes can fix that, and refusing to hand over the document
  would help nobody.
\end{description}
When the compiling command reports success, we take its word for it.
latexmk(1) succeeds for a document that settled even if a file it asked for could
not be made, for instance when [[\bibliography]] names a file that doesn't
exist, so such a document counts as compiled here too.

The limit is seven passes, kept in [[TEX_MAX_PASSES]].
latexmk(1) stops after five by default, which can be too few for a document
using the [[footnotereuse]] option of the didactic package, whose footnotes need
several passes to settle.
The one number drives two loops, because which of them does the work depends on
the command.
latexmk(1) reruns LaTeX itself, so its limit is its [[$max_repeat]] variable.
A plain [[pdflatex]] runs once per call, so there the shell loop below is the
only one.
A project that needs another limit sets [[TEX_MAX_PASSES]]: a [[$max_repeat]] in
a [[latexmkrc]] file has no effect, since the one on the command line here
overrides it.
<<variables>>=
TEX_MAX_PASSES?=  7
LATEX?=           latexmk -dvi -use-make -8bit -e '$$max_repeat=${TEX_MAX_PASSES}'
PDFLATEX?=        latexmk -pdf -use-make -8bit -e '$$max_repeat=${TEX_MAX_PASSES}'
LATEXFLAGS?=
PREPROCESS.tex?=  ${PDFLATEX} ${LATEXFLAGS} $<
PREPROCESS.dtx?=  ${PREPROCESS.tex}
TEX_OUTDIR?=      ltxobj
COMPILE.tex?=     $(call TEX_COMPILE,${PDFLATEX})
COMPILE.dtx?=     ${COMPILE.tex}
@ Thus one would have to change the [[COMPILE.tex]] variable to change format
from PDF to DVI unless a suffix rule is used.

[[TEX_COMPILE]] takes the compiling command as its argument, so that PDF and DVI
share the loop and with it the outcomes above.
Its commands all run in one shell, which is what lets a failure in any of them
end the recipe with its own status: a recipe otherwise reports the status of its
\emph{last} command, here the warning, and that succeeds however the compilation
went.
That is how failed builds used to go unnoticed.
\Cref{tex:Tests} tests these outcomes.

The loop stops when the passes are used up, when latexmk(1) has given up, or when
the log no longer asks for another pass.
A latexmk(1) that has given up gets exactly one more run, because it gives up
before the later steps of a chain: with [[-xelatex]] the PDF is made from the
[[.xdv]] file by xdvipdfmx(1), which it hasn't reached, so without that run there
would be no PDF at all.
That run normally doesn't start LaTeX over, because latexmk(1) marks the rule as
done when it gives up and records the state of the files it generated; so it goes
straight to the remaining steps, and where the output is direct, as with plain
[[-pdf]], it answers \enquote{Nothing to do}.
More runs would be pointless.
It does start over if one of the document's own input files has changed since,
since that record covers only generated files: the document may rewrite it
itself, or someone may save an edit during the build.
Go mode, [[-g]] or [[$go_mode]], also makes it start over, since that is what it
asks for.
It then gives up again before the same step, which is why the finishing run is
followed by a check that there is any output at all.
We look for \enquote{Rerun to get} rather than for the cross-references alone,
since [[hyperref]] words its request for another pass over the outlines the same
way.
A table of contents that is merely out of date prints no such request, so a plain
[[pdflatex]] can stop too early for it, while latexmk(1), which compares the
files themselves, will not.
<<variables>>=
TEX_COMPILE?=     \
  TEX_UNSETTLED=; i=1; \
  $(call TEX_RUN,$(1)); ${CHECK_TEX_RUN}; \
  while [ "$$i" -lt ${TEX_MAX_PASSES} ] && [ -z "$$TEX_UNSETTLED" ] && \
        grep -q "Rerun to get" ${TEX_LOG}; do \
    i=$$((i+1)); \
    $(call TEX_RUN,$(1)); ${CHECK_TEX_RUN}; \
  done; \
  if [ -n "$$TEX_UNSETTLED" ]; then \
    $(call TEX_RUN,$(1)); ${CHECK_TEX_RUN}; ${CHECK_TEX_OUTPUT}; \
  fi; \
  ${WARN_UNSETTLED}
@

Each run is wrapped so that its standard error reaches both the terminal and a
file we can read afterwards, since the status alone turns out not to be enough.
Standard error is where latexmk(1) and make(1) say what we need to know.
We leave standard output alone, connected to the terminal, because \TeX{} would
buffer it if it were a pipe: a document stuck in a loop would then show nothing
at all.
\TeX{} also still gets the terminal as its input, so it can still stop at an
error and ask.
File descriptor 3 is how the inner command reaches that standard output from
inside the pipeline.
The status has to be put aside explicitly, because a pipeline reports the status
of tee(1).
The pipeline ends in [[|| :]] so that a shell running with [[-e]] can't end the
recipe here, before we have looked at what kind of failure it was: with
[[pipefail]] the failure would otherwise be the pipeline's, and without the
[[||]] the group could stop before writing the status at all.
We remove the previous status first, so that a shell killed before it could write
a new one isn't read as this run having succeeded.
We create [[TEX_OUTDIR]] first, for two reasons: tee(1) has to be able to create
its file there, and while latexmk(1) would create the directory itself, a plain
[[pdflatex]] refuses to write there and stops at once.
The [[$(or ...)]] is for [[TEX_OUTDIR]] set to nothing at all: everything else
in this file then addresses [[/]], and these two files should not be what
first notices.

We name the log by the base name of the source rather than by substituting
[[.tex]] with [[.log]], since that substitution does nothing to a [[.dtx]] file:
the loop would then look for the message in the source file, or in a file that
doesn't exist, and never ask for a second pass.
<<variables>>=
TEX_LOG?=         ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<)).log
TEX_RUNDIR?=      $(or ${TEX_OUTDIR},.)
TEX_RUNLOG?=      ${TEX_RUNDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<)).texrunlog
TEX_RUNSTATUS?=   ${TEX_RUNDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<)).texrunstatus
TEX_RUN?=         ${MKDIR} ${TEX_RUNDIR}; ${RM} ${TEX_RUNSTATUS}; \
  { { $(1) ${LATEXFLAGS} -output-directory=${TEX_OUTDIR} $<; \
      echo $$? > ${TEX_RUNSTATUS}; } 2>&1 >&3 | tee ${TEX_RUNLOG} >&2 || :; } 3>&1
@

The status isn't enough because latexmk(1) spends a single one, 12, on both a
broken document and an unsettled one.
It tells them apart only in words: for the latter it reports \enquote{Maximum
runs of \ldots{} reached without getting stable files}.
We therefore treat a failure as an unsettled document only when latexmk(1) says
it ran out of runs and no recipe failed.
The second [[grep]] is what keeps this from masking failures again: a run whose
[[-use-make]] delegation ran a rule that failed leaves make(1)'s
[[*** [...] Error]], or [[*** [...] Terminated]] if the recipe was killed.
We look for a \emph{recipe} that failed rather than for make(1) being unhappy in
general, since latexmk(1) asks it for every file LaTeX reported missing,
including the ones LaTeX is about to write itself, and make(1) answers
\enquote{No rule to make target} with nothing at all wrong.

Force mode, which a project can turn on with [[-f]] or in its [[latexmkrc]], looks
unsettled at first, since latexmk(1) then carries on past an error until it runs
out of runs too.
The finishing run above tells them apart: latexmk(1) remembers the error from the
invocation before and fails again, this time without saying it ran out of runs,
and then the status counts.
That depends on the finishing run not starting over.
If it does, latexmk(1) just runs out of runs again and a broken document passes
for an unsettled one: with [[-f -g]] at once, and with direct output for a document
that rewrites its own input, on the [[make]] after the one that failed.
Under [[-xelatex]] the latter has no output, so the check for that catches it.

We accept that rather than search the log for \TeX's errors, which would be the
obvious way to catch these.
An error does start a line with an exclamation mark, but so can other text, because
the log wraps at 79 columns: the documentation of nytid, which has no error at all,
has such a line in the middle of an overfull box, and would fail.

A plain [[pdflatex]] never says it ran out of runs, so any failure of it counts
as a broken document, as it should.

A broken document also loses its target and latexmk(1)'s database.
\TeX{} ships what it has, so a failed run can leave a fresh output file behind,
and the next make(1) would answer that it is up to date and succeed.
Without the database, latexmk(1) has to work out again what needs doing; with it,
it could find nothing changed since it last tried and answer \enquote{Nothing to
do}, although what failed has not been fixed.
What goes is [[$@]], usually the link in the working directory; the output in
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] stays, for reading what got that far.
That output does still count as up to date if something asks for it by that name,
as [[make ltxobj/d.pdf]] after a failed [[make d.pdf]] would.
<<variables>>=
CHECK_TEX_RUN?= \
  rc=$$(cat ${TEX_RUNSTATUS} 2>/dev/null) || rc=1; [ -n "$$rc" ] || rc=1; \
  if [ "$$rc" = 0 ]; then :; \
  elif grep -q '^Latexmk: .*without getting stable files' ${TEX_RUNLOG} && \
       ! grep -qE '^make(\[[0-9]+\])?: \*\*\* \[' ${TEX_RUNLOG}; \
  then \
    TEX_UNSETTLED=yes; \
  else \
    ${RM} $@ ${TEX_RUNDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<)).fdb_latexmk; exit $$rc; \
  fi
@

A document that didn't settle succeeds on the understanding that there is a
document to hand over, so that is what the check after the finishing run asks for.
It names the output after the source rather than using [[$@]], because some
projects build [[ltxobj/slides-incomplete.pdf]] from [[slides.tex]] and move the
output afterwards.
A file left by an earlier build also satisfies it.
<<variables>>=
CHECK_TEX_OUTPUT?= \
  [ -s ${TEX_RUNDIR}/$(basename $(notdir $<))$(suffix $@) ] || { \
    echo "tex.mk: $< did not settle and left no output" >&2; \
    ${RM} $@; exit 1; }
@

The warning comes after the loop so that a document gets one warning however it
got there: latexmk(1) said so, or a plain [[pdflatex]] used up its passes and
the log still asks for another.
It must not promise more than it knows.
A document may have no fixed point, or it may merely need more passes than it
got.
<<variables>>=
WARN_UNSETTLED?= \
  if [ -n "$$TEX_UNSETTLED" ] || grep -q "Rerun to get" ${TEX_LOG}; then \
    echo "tex.mk: WARNING: $< did not settle within the limit of passes;" \
      "its references may be off, and its log may hold errors" >&2; \
  fi
@

%We note that we add this output directory to the search path for 
%prerequisites~\cite[see][Sect.\ 4.5.1]{GNUMake}.
%The reason for this is that we might need some out the objects as prerequisites 
%for other files.

Normally, the above is all that is needed.
However, if you need to manually build the bibliography, you can either add the
[[.bbl]] file as a prerequisite or set the following variable to a non-empty 
string.
<<variables>>=
TEX_BBL?=
@ Similarly as for the main LaTeX commands, the bibtex(1) command is controlled 
by
<<variables>>=
BIBTEX?=            bibtexu
BIBTEXFLAGS?=
BIBLIOGRAPHY.aux?=  ${BIBTEX} ${BIBTEXFLAGS} $< || [ $$? = 1 ]
@ bibtex(1) exits with 1 after a mere warning, such as an entry that lacks a
publisher, and with 2 or more after an error.
Only the latter should fail the rule, since a failed rule under [[-use-make]] is
what tells a broken document from an unsettled one in \cref{tex:Intro}.
The warning has already been printed by then.

And, in case we use biblatex, the biber(1) command is controlled by
<<variables>>=
BIBER?=             biber
BIBERFLAGS?=
BIBLIOGRAPHY.bcf?=  ${BIBER} -O $@ ${BIBERFLAGS} $<
@

Similarly as for the bibliography, to enable indexing you can either manually 
add the [[.ind]] file as a prerequisite, or you can set the following variable 
to a non-empty string.
<<variables>>=
TEX_IND?=
@ The indexing-related programs are the following.
<<variables>>=
XINDY?=       texindy
XINDYFLAGS?=
COMPILE.idx?= ${XINDY} ${OUTPUT_OPTION} ${XINDYFLAGS} $<

MAKEINDEX?=   makeindex
MAKEIDXFLAGS?=
COMPILE.nlo?= ${MAKEINDEX} ${OUTPUT_OPTION} ${MAKEIDXFLAGS} -s nomencl.ist $<
@

We also provide support for PythonTeX.
This is enabled by the following variable.
<<variables>>=
TEX_PYTHONTEX?=
@ Then the required command and flags are controlled with the following 
variables.
<<variables>>=
PYTHONTEX?=       python3 $$(which pythontex)
PYTHONTEXFLAGS?=  --interpreter python:python3
@

Finally, we provide targets to easily add external classes as dependencies.
We add the phony targets
\begin{itemize}
\item[[lncs]] for Springer \ac{LNCS},
\item[[biblatex-lncs]] for the \ac{LNCS} bibliography style for the 
[[biblatex]] package,
\item[[rfc]] or [[rfc.bib]] for an up-to-date bibliography containing all 
\ac{IETF} \acp{RFC},
\item[[popets]] for the style files of the Proceedings of the Privacy Enhancing 
Technologies (PoPETS, De Gruyter Open/Sciendo).
\end{itemize}

Finally, we add a method to quickly merge bibliographies without redundancy.
This is based on the archive syntax of make(1).
This allows us to have the resulting full bibliography [[full.bib]] consist of 
the subbibliographies [[sub0.bib]] and [[sub1.bib]]:
\begin{lstlisting}
full.bib: full.bib(sub0.bib sub1.bib)
\end{lstlisting}
Or, directly as a dependency for the PDF:
\begin{lstlisting}
paper.pdf: full.bib(sub0.bib sub1.bib)
\end{lstlisting}


\section{Implementation overview}

The structure of the include file is similar to a header file in C or C++.
The include file uses the old C-style technique to prevent multiple inclusions.
<<[[tex.mk]]>>=
ifndef TEX_MK
TEX_MK=true

.NOTPARALLEL:

INCLUDE_MAKEFILES?=.
include ${INCLUDE_MAKEFILES}/portability.mk

<<variables>>
<<targets for documents>>
<<targets for class and package files>>
<<targets for external classes>>
<<targets for cleaning>>

endif
@ We include [[portability.mk]] (\cref{portability.mk}) to get portable 
settings for several common utilities.
It is currently not possible to run the TeX-builds in parallel, hence the need 
for the [[.NOTPARALLEL]] target --- this runs any jobs that includes [[tex.mk]] 
  in serial.
However, this is not inherited, any recursive make invocations will run in 
parallel unless [[.NOTPARALLEL]] is specified there as well.

We will start with the targets for cleaning.
We provide two phony targets, [[clean-tex]] and [[distclean-tex]], and we 
add them as prerequisites to [[clean]] and [[distclean]], respectively.
<<targets for cleaning>>=
.PHONY: clean clean-tex
clean: clean-tex

clean-tex:
	<<clean recipe>>

.PHONY: distclean distclean-tex
distclean: distclean-tex

distclean-tex:
	<<distclean recipe>>
@ We will add to the recipes in the remainder of the chapter.
However, as latexmk(1) is set as the default in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can 
already add the following line to the cleaning recipe.
<<clean recipe>>=
-latexmk -C -output-directory=${TEX_OUTDIR}
[ "${TEX_OUTDIR}" -ef "$$(pwd)" ] || \
  ${RM} -R ${TEX_OUTDIR}
${RM} *.pytxcode
${RM} -R pythontex-files-*
@ The output directory may be the working directory, and latexmk(1)'s cleaning
doesn't know about the copies of the output we keep in \cref{tex:Intro}.
<<clean recipe>>=
${RM} ${TEX_RUNDIR}/*.texrunlog ${TEX_RUNDIR}/*.texrunstatus
@


\section{Targets for documents}

Now we will treat how to compile documents.
<<targets for documents>>=
<<auxillary files>>
<<bibliography files>>
<<indices files>>
<<PythonTeX files>>
<<document files>>
<<target for latexmkrc>>
@ These will be discussed in the following sections.
However, since we use latexmk(1) by default (\cref{tex:Intro}), we will discuss 
the relevant [[<<latexmkrc>>]] entries in parallel.
We supply a target to easily use our [[<<latexmkrc>>]] with latexmk(1).
<<target for latexmkrc>>=
latexmkrc:
	[ -e $@ -o "${INCLUDE_MAKEFILES}" = "." ] || \
	${LN} -s ${INCLUDE_MAKEFILES}/latexmkrc $@
@ We also add the corresponding line for cleaning.
<<distclean recipe>>=
[ ! -L latexmkrc ] || ${RM} latexmkrc
@

We will now discuss the different files we need latex(1) to generate.
Note that in many cases we want latex(1) to generate more files, \eg [[.toc]]
files, but we do not have to care about these here.
The reason we can ignore those files is that they do not require any external 
tool, \eg bibtex(1), to be run, these files just requires another run of 
latex(1).

\subsection{Auxillary files}

Many steps in compiling a LaTeX document needs the [[.aux]] file.
Thus we will first introduce a rule for creating the [[.aux]] file.
We will create it in the specified output directory.
<<auxillary files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>
@ Then we simply do
<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>=
${MKDIR} ${TEX_OUTDIR}
${PREPROCESS.tex}
@

\subsection{Bibliographies}

One file that is commonly needed is the one used to create the bibliography, 
the [[.bbl]] file.
There are two ways to create this file, either using classical bibtex(1) or 
using the [[biblatex]] package and biber(1).
<<bibliography files>>=
<<bbl target for bibtex>>
<<bbl target for biber>>
@

The first approach, using bibtex(1), depends on the [[.aux]] file.
This means that we can have a target creating the desired [[.bbl]] file from 
the [[.aux]] file.
bibtex(1) writes its [[.bbl]] and [[.blg]] beside the [[.aux]] file it is given,
so they are already where the target says and there is nothing to move.
<<bbl target for bibtex>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bbl: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux
	${BIBLIOGRAPHY.aux}
@

The second approach uses the [[biblatex]] package~\cite{biblatex} and 
biber(1)~\cite{biber}.
They do not rely on the [[.aux]] file, instead [[biblatex]] creates a [[.bcf]] 
file.
Thus its target is exactly the same as that of the [[.aux]] file.
<<bbl target for biber>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bcf: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>
@ This [[.bcf]] file is in turn used by biber(1) to create the [[.bbl]] file.
To compile the [[.bbl]] with biber(1) and put the output files in the desired
directory, we do the following.
<<bbl target for biber>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bbl: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bcf
	${BIBLIOGRAPHY.bcf}
@

As mentioned in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can automatically add the [[.bbl]] file as
a prerequisite if the variable [[TEX_BBL]] is set.
<<bibliography files>>=
ifneq (${TEX_BBL},)
%.pdf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bbl
endif
@

\subsection{Indices}

There are several time we need to work with indices, \eg when working with 
standard indices but also glossaries.
Here we provide some suffix rules to make it easier to build such indices.

Before we start, however, we will note that many of these rules are not needed 
if the [[imakeidx]] package~\cite{imakeidx} is used.
We do recommend to use this package.
Furthermore, we provide rules for the [[nomencl]] package, however, we 
recommend to use the [[glossaries]] package~\cite{glossaries} instead.
The [[glossaries]] package also has native support for xindy(1).
Although the [[glossaries]] package supports abbreviations and acronyms, we 
recommend the [[acro]] package~\cite{acro} for this instead.

The standard LaTeX index uses an [[.idx]] file, which is generated similarly as
the [[.aux]] file.
Thus we can use the same type of target.
<<indices files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.idx: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>
@ The actual index, which resides in a [[.ind]] file, can then be generated as 
follows.
<<indices files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.ind: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.idx
	${COMPILE.idx}
@ As mentioned in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can automatically add the [[.ind]] file 
as
a prerequisite if the variable [[TEX_IND]] is set.
<<indices files>>=
ifneq (${TEX_IND},)
%.pdf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.ind
endif
@

For backwards compatibility, we provide the following code for the 
[[nomenclature]] package.
<<indices files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.nlo: %.tex
	<<create output directory and preprocess TeX>>

${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.nls: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.nlo
	${COMPILE.nlo}
@ And now we add the corresponding code for latexmk(1).
The code is fetched from the latexmk example-files on \ac{CTAN}\footnote{%
  URL: \url{http://mirrors.ctan.org/support/latexmk/example_rcfiles/nomenclature_latexmkrc}
}.
<<[[latexmkrc]]>>=
add_cus_dep( 'nlo', 'nls', 0, 'makenlo2nls' );
sub makenlo2nls {
	system( "makeindex -s nomencl.ist -o \"$_[0].nls\" \"$_[0].nlo\"" );
}
@

\subsection{PythonTeX}

Occasionally we use PythonTeX.
We also provide a target for the required files.
However, this construction requires that we load PythonTeX as follows.
\begin{minted}{LaTeX}
\usepackage{pythontex}
\setpythontexoutputdir{.}
\setpythontexworkingdir{..}
\end{minted}

The code is as follows.
<<PythonTeX files>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxcode: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux
	cd $(dir $@) && ${PYTHONTEX} ${PYTHONTEXFLAGS} $(basename $(notdir $@))
%.pytxmcr ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxmcr:: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxcode
	cd ${TEX_OUTDIR} && ${PYTHONTEX} ${PYTHONTEXFLAGS} $(basename $(notdir $@))
@

As mentioned in \cref{tex:Intro}, we can automatically add the [[.pytxmcr]] 
file as a prerequisite if the variable [[TEX_PYTHONTEX]] is set.
<<bibliography files>>=
ifneq (${TEX_PYTHONTEX},)
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pytxmcr
endif
@

If we use latexmk(1), then we must also add instructions for this in 
[[<<latexmkrc>>]].
The following code is fetched from the latexmk example-files on 
\ac{CTAN}\footnote{%
  URL: \url{http://mirrors.ctan.org/support/latexmk/example_rcfiles/pythontex-latexmkrc}
}.
<<[[latexmkrc]]>>=
#  This version has a fudge on the latex and pdflatex commands that
#  allows the pythontex custom dependency to work even when $out_dir
#  is used to set the output directory.  Without the fudge (done by
#  trickery symbolic links) the custom dependency for using pythontex
#  will not be detected.

add_cus_dep('pytxcode', 'pytxmcr', 0, 'pythontex');
sub pythontex {
    # This subroutine is a fudge, because it from latexmk's point of
    # view, it makes the main .tex file depend on the .pytxcode file.
    # But it doesn't actually make the .tex file, but is used for its
    # side effects in creating other files.  The dependence is a way
    # of triggering the rule to be run whenever the .pytxcode file
    # changes, and to do this before running latex/pdflatex again.
    return system("python3 \$(which pythontex) --interpreter python:python3 --verbose \"$_[0]\"");
}

$pdflatex = 'internal mylatex %R %Z pdflatex %O %S';
$latex = 'internal mylatex %R %Z latex %O %S';
sub mylatex {
   my $root = shift;
   my $dir_string = shift;
   my $code = "$root.pytxcode";
   my $result = "pythontex-files-$root";
   if ($dir_string) {
      warn "mylatex: Making symlinks to fool cus_dep creation\n";
      unlink $code;
      if (-l $result) {
          unlink $result;
      }
      elsif (-d $result) {
         unlink glob "$result/*";
         rmdir $result;
      }
      symlink $dir_string.$code, $code;
      if ( ! -e $dir_string.$result ) { mkdir $dir_string.$result; }
      symlink $dir_string.$result, $result;
   }
   else {
      foreach ($code, $result) { if (-l) { unlink; } }
   }
   return system @_;
}
@

\subsection{Document files}

Now that we have all prerequisite files, we can actually compile the document.
For simplicity we add file in both the current working directory and the 
[[TEX_OUTDIR]] as targets.
The reason for this is that it makes the makefile easier to write, usually we 
prefer writing just the [[.pdf]] file --- and not the path to the [[.pdf]] in 
the [[TEX_OUTDIR]] directory.
And for the same reason, we create a symbolic link between them after 
compilation --- this allows make(1) to track modification times correctly.
(We need a symbolic link, rather than a hard link, because upon recompilation, 
the file in [[TEX_OUTDIR]] is unlinked, whereas the other is not.)
<<document files>>=
%.pdf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: %.tex
	${COMPILE.tex}
	-${LN} ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@ $@

%.dvi ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.dvi: %.tex
	<<compile DVI>>
	-${LN} ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@ $@
@

The compilation step for DVI is that of [[COMPILE.tex]] above, with the same
three outcomes and the same limit, but with [[LATEX]] as the command.
<<compile DVI>>=
$(call TEX_COMPILE,${LATEX})
@


\section{Testing the compilation}\label{tex:Tests}

The three outcomes of \cref{tex:Intro} rest on how latexmk(1), \TeX{} and make(1)
behave together, and a mistake here doesn't show: a build that wrongly succeeds
looks just like one that rightly does.
So [[test-tex.sh]] checks the outcomes, run by [[make check]]
(\cref{Makefile}).

It builds real documents with the real tools.
That makes it take minutes, but the alternative is to test against our own idea
of what these tools do, and that idea has been wrong often enough: latexmk(1)'s
wording, where its messages go and what it does when it has given up were all
found out by running it.
It replaces a tool only to get a failure nothing else produces, and the prose
says so where that happens.
Even so, small documents only go so far.
Two fixes to this code passed on small documents and would have broken the
documentation of nytid, so a change to the compilation should also be tried on a
large document.

The script needs GNU make(1), latexmk(1), a \TeX{} distribution with XeTeX,
bibtexu(1), biber(1), pdftotext(1) and the [[script]] command of util-linux, so it
is for Linux.
It takes the directory holding [[tex.mk]] as an optional argument, so that a
modified copy can be tested, for instance to check that a test fails when the
code it covers is broken.
Each case runs in its own directory of a temporary directory, which is removed
if all the tests pass and otherwise kept for reading the output.
<<[[test-tex.sh]]>>=
#!/bin/bash
<<test setup>>
<<test helpers>>
<<test cases>>
<<test report>>
@

We clear make(1)'s variables because [[make check]] would otherwise pass its own
flags on to every build in here.
<<test setup>>=
MK=$(cd "${1:-$(dirname "$0")}" && pwd) || exit 1
W=$(mktemp -d) || exit 1
unset MAKEFLAGS MFLAGS MAKELEVEL
mkdir "$W/mk"
cp "$MK"/tex.mk "$MK"/portability.mk "$MK"/latexmkrc "$W/mk/" || exit 1
FAILS=0
NF=-interaction=nonstopmode
INF=100000
@

The script must report failure in its own status, or we would repeat in the tests
the mistake they are there to catch.
<<test report>>=
echo
if [ "$FAILS" = 0 ]; then
  echo "ALL OK"
  rm -rf "$W"
else
  echo "$FAILS FAILED, see $W"
  exit 1
fi
@

To see how many passes are needed, and to control it, a document counts through a
label.
Each pass reads the previous value, so [[doc u 3]] settles on the fourth pass, and
[[doc u $INF]] doesn't settle within any limit we would use.
This is what makes the \enquote{did not settle} outcome testable at all.
<<test helpers>>=
newcase(){ # directory: start a case, with a Makefile that includes our tex.mk
  mkdir -p "$W/$1"; cd "$W/$1" || exit 1
  printf 'all:\n\nINCLUDE_MAKEFILES=../mk\ninclude ../mk/tex.mk\n' > Makefile
}
doc(){ # name target-value preamble body
  cat > "$1.tex" <<EOF
\\documentclass{article}
\\usepackage{refcount}
$3
\\makeatletter
\\newcounter{c}
\\begin{document}
\\setcounter{c}{\\getrefnumber{v}}
\\ifnum\\value{c}<$2 \\stepcounter{c}\\fi
Value \\arabic{c}.
\\protected@edef\\@currentlabel{\\arabic{c}}\\label{v}
\\makeatother
$4
\\end{document}
EOF
}
plain(){ # file body preamble: a document without the counter
  printf '\\documentclass{article}%s\\begin{document}%s\\end{document}\n' \
    "$3" "$2" > "$1"
}
GOOD='Good.'
BROKEN='\undefinedmacro{} Bad.'
@

Every case goes through the same call of make(1), with no terminal as input and
no prompt to get stuck at, and leaves its status in [[RC]].
<<test helpers>>=
m(){ # output-file make-args...
  o=$1; shift
  timeout 280 make "$@" LATEXFLAGS=$NF </dev/null >"$o" 2>&1; RC=$?
}
passes(){ grep -cE '^This is (pdfTeX|XeTeX)' "$1"; }
warned(){ grep -c '^tex.mk: WARNING' "$1"; }
exists(){ [ -s "$1" ] && echo yes || echo no; }
is(){ # label want got
  if [ "$2" = "$3" ]; then
    printf 'ok    %-44s %s\n' "$1" "$3"
  else
    printf 'FAIL  %-44s want %s got %s\n' "$1" "$2" "$3"
    FAILS=$((FAILS+1))
  fi
}
@

\subsection{A failed build stays failed}

\TeX{} ships what it has, so a failed run can leave a fresh output file, and if
that made the next make(1) succeed we would be back where we started.
Hence every case runs make(1) twice.
The [[sleep]] is there because make(1) compares modification times.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== a failed build keeps failing on the next make"
newcase rerun1; plain d.tex "$GOOD"
m o0 d.pdf; is "good first build rc" 0 "$RC"
sleep 1; plain d.tex "$BROKEN"
m o1 d.pdf; is "edited to break, run 1 rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 d.pdf; is "edited to break, run 2 rc" 2 "$RC"
is "the partial PDF stays readable in TEX_OUTDIR" yes "$(exists ltxobj/d.pdf)"
newcase rerun2; plain u.tex "$BROKEN"
m o1 ltxobj/u.pdf; is "prefixed target, run 1 rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 ltxobj/u.pdf; is "prefixed target, run 2 rc" 2 "$RC"
newcase rerun3; plain u.tex "$BROKEN"
m o1 ltxobj/u.pdf PDFLATEX=pdflatex; is "prefixed, pdflatex, run 1 rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 ltxobj/u.pdf PDFLATEX=pdflatex; is "prefixed, pdflatex, run 2 rc" 2 "$RC"
newcase rerun4; plain u.tex "$BROKEN"
m o1 ltxobj/u.dvi; is "prefixed DVI, run 1 rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 ltxobj/u.dvi; is "prefixed DVI, run 2 rc" 2 "$RC"
@

The status of the previous run must not be mistaken for that of this one.
A [[-e]] shell tests whether a failure ends the recipe before the status is
written.
For the other way to lose a status, a shell killed before it can write one, we
must inject the failure, since nothing else kills that shell and leaves make(1)
running.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== a stale status cannot pass a broken document (-e shell)"
newcase errexit; plain d.tex "$GOOD"
m o0 d.pdf .SHELLFLAGS=-ec; is "good build under -ec rc" 0 "$RC"
sleep 1; plain d.tex "$BROKEN"
m o1 d.pdf .SHELLFLAGS=-ec; is "broken after good under -ec rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 d.pdf .SHELLFLAGS=-ec; is "  and on the next make" 2 "$RC"

echo "=== a shell killed before writing its status is not an old success"
newcase killed; plain d.tex "$GOOD"
m o0 d.pdf; is "good first build rc" 0 "$RC"
sleep 1; plain d.tex 'Edited.'
m o1 d.pdf 'PDFLATEX=sh -c "kill -9 \$$PPID" x'
is "wrapping shell killed rc" 2 "$RC"
@

\subsection{A document that doesn't settle}

The opposite mistake is to fail a document that only ran out of passes.
Under [[-xelatex]] and [[-pdfdvi]] the PDF is made after the point where latexmk(1)
gives up, so there the check that the PDF exists is what shows the finishing run
doing its job.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== unsettled documents warn; only a real failure fails"
newcase uns; doc u $INF
m o u.pdf; is "plain unsettled rc" 0 "$RC"
is "plain unsettled warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
is "plain unsettled passes" 7 "$(passes o)"
is "one relaunch after giving up" 1 "$(grep -c 'Nothing to do' o)"
is "the output exists" yes "$(exists ltxobj/u.pdf)"
newcase unsxe; doc u $INF
m o u.pdf "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -xelatex -use-make -8bit -e '\$\$max_repeat=7'"
is "unsettled, -xelatex rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
is "  passes" 7 "$(passes o)"
is "  the PDF exists (made by the finishing run)" yes "$(exists ltxobj/u.pdf)"
newcase unspd; doc u $INF
m o u.pdf "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdfdvi -use-make -8bit -e '\$\$max_repeat=7'"
is "unsettled, -pdfdvi rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
is "  the PDF exists (made by the finishing run)" yes "$(exists ltxobj/u.pdf)"
@

A bibliography is the step most likely to run through make(1) inside a document
that doesn't settle, and bibtex(1) has a status that means no harm: 1, for
warnings only.
A real bibtex(1) error makes latexmk(1) report failure by itself, so only an
injected status of 2 shows what our rule does with one.

The first known limit is here too: the failed step left a [[.bbl]] file, so on
the next make(1) nothing asks for it again, and the document counts as unsettled.
We assert that as it stands, labelled [[KNOWN LIMIT]], so that a change to it is
noticed; issue 100 in the tracker is about these limits.
<<test cases>>=
BIBOK='@book{a,author={A Author},title={A Title},publisher={P},year=2000}'
BIBWARN='@book{a,author={A Author},title={A Title},year=2000}'
CLASSIC='See \cite{a}.\bibliographystyle{plain}\bibliography{r}'
BIBLATEX='\usepackage[backend=biber]{biblatex}\addbibresource{r.bib}'
HAS_BIB_SWITCH=; grep -q '^TEX_BIB?=' "$W/mk/tex.mk" && HAS_BIB_SWITCH=yes
BB=; [ -n "$HAS_BIB_SWITCH" ] && BB=TEX_BIB=bibtex

newcase unsbib; doc u $INF '' "$CLASSIC"; echo "$BIBOK" > r.bib
m o u.pdf $BB; is "unsettled, classical bib clean rc" 0 "$RC"
is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
newcase unsbibw; doc u $INF '' "$CLASSIC"; echo "$BIBWARN" > r.bib
m o u.pdf $BB; is "unsettled, classical bib with warning rc" 0 "$RC"
is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
is "  the BibTeX warning is still printed" yes \
  "$(grep -q 'Warning--' o && echo yes || echo no)"
newcase unsbibe; echo "$BIBOK" > r.bib
doc u $INF '' 'See \cite{a}.\bibliographystyle{nosuchstyle}\bibliography{r}'
m o u.pdf $BB; is "unsettled, bibtex real error (no style) rc" 2 "$RC"
newcase unsbib2; doc u $INF '' "$CLASSIC"; echo "$BIBOK" > r.bib
printf '#!/bin/sh\nbibtexu "$@"\nexit 2\n' > fb; chmod +x fb
m o u.pdf $BB BIBTEX=./fb; is "unsettled, bibtex exits 2 (injected) rc" 2 "$RC"
m o u.pdf $BB BIBTEX=./fb
is "  KNOWN LIMIT next make, .bbl left behind" 0 "$RC"; is "    warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
newcase bibwset; plain s.tex "$CLASSIC"; echo "$BIBWARN" > r.bib
m o s.pdf $BB; is "settled, classical bib with warning rc" 0 "$RC"
is "  warned" 0 "$(warned o)"
is "  citation resolved" yes \
  "$(pdftotext ltxobj/s.pdf - 2>/dev/null | grep -q 'See \[1\]' && echo yes || echo no)"
@

The cases for biblatex need [[TEX_BIB]] to select the biber rule.
Without it, the [[bibtexu]] sub-make that latexmk(1) runs for such a document
fails, harmlessly, and that is a failed recipe to us.
<<test cases>>=
if [ -n "$HAS_BIB_SWITCH" ]; then
  newcase unsblx; doc u $INF "$BIBLATEX" 'See \cite{a}.\printbibliography'
  echo "$BIBOK" > r.bib
  m o u.pdf; is "unsettled, biblatex rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
  newcase bblfail; doc u $INF "$BIBLATEX" 'See \cite{a}.\printbibliography'
  echo "$BIBOK" > r.bib
  m o u.pdf 'BIBLIOGRAPHY.bcf=exit 2'; is "unsettled, biber recipe fails rc" 2 "$RC"
  m o u.pdf 'BIBLIOGRAPHY.bcf=exit 2'
  is "  KNOWN LIMIT next make, .bbl left behind" 0 "$RC"
  is "    warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
else
  echo "(biblatex + unsettled skipped: this tex.mk has no TEX_BIB)"
fi
@

Any recipe latexmk(1) runs through [[-use-make]] can fail, and it can fail in two
ways that make(1) words differently: with [[Error]], or with [[Terminated]] when
it's killed.
A figure built by the same [[tex.mk]] checks that this isn't overdone: its
sub-make prints a compilation of its own, which must not confuse the verdict on
the document around it.
<<test cases>>=
INPUT_GEN='\InputIfFileExists{gen.tex}{}{\typeout{No file gen.tex.}}'
newcase recfail; doc u $INF '' "$INPUT_GEN"; printf 'gen.tex:\n\tfalse\n' >> Makefile
m o u.pdf; is "unsettled, recipe fails rc" 2 "$RC"
m o u.pdf; is "  and on the next make" 2 "$RC"
newcase recsig; doc u $INF '' "$INPUT_GEN"
printf 'gen.tex:\n\tkill -TERM $$$$\n' >> Makefile
m o u.pdf; is "unsettled, recipe killed by signal rc" 2 "$RC"
m o u.pdf; is "  and on the next make" 2 "$RC"
m o u.pdf; is "  and the one after" 2 "$RC"

echo "=== a sub-make echoing tex.mk's own recipe does not confuse the classifier"
FIG='\includegraphics{fig}'
GRAPHICX='\usepackage{graphicx}'
newcase nest1; doc u $INF "$GRAPHICX" "$FIG"; plain fig.tex F
m o u.pdf; is "unsettled, nested figure rc" 0 "$RC"
is "  warned for main" 1 "$(grep -c '^tex.mk: WARNING: u.tex' o)"
newcase nest2; plain u.tex "$FIG\\undefinedmacro{}" "$GRAPHICX"; plain fig.tex F
m o u.pdf; is "broken, nested figure rc" 2 "$RC"

echo "=== plain pdflatex notices every rerun request LaTeX words the same way"
newcase hy; plain h.tex '\tableofcontents\section{A}' '\usepackage{hyperref}'
m o h.pdf PDFLATEX=pdflatex; is "hyperref rc" 0 "$RC"
is "  passes" 2 "$(passes o)"; is "  warned" 0 "$(warned o)"
@

\subsection{The limit}

Two documents straddle the limit: one settles on the last pass allowed, the other
would on the first pass too many.
Both tools must agree on where it lies, for PDF and for DVI, since one number
drives two loops.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== the limit has one name"
newcase lim; doc k6 6; doc k7 7
for tool in latexmk pdflatex; do
  a=; [ $tool = pdflatex ] && a=PDFLATEX=pdflatex
  rm -rf ltxobj k6.pdf k7.pdf
  m o k6.pdf $a; is "$tool k6 passes" 7 "$(passes o)"; is "  warned" 0 "$(warned o)"
  m o k7.pdf $a; is "$tool k7 passes" 7 "$(passes o)"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
  is "  rc" 0 "$RC"
  rm -rf ltxobj k7.pdf
  m o k7.pdf $a TEX_MAX_PASSES=9
  is "$tool k7 TEX_MAX_PASSES=9 passes" 8 "$(passes o)"; is "  warned" 0 "$(warned o)"
done
newcase limd; doc k6 6; doc k7 7
for tool in latexmk latex; do
  a=; [ $tool = latex ] && a=LATEX=latex
  rm -rf ltxobj k6.dvi k7.dvi
  m o k6.dvi $a; is "$tool DVI k6 passes" 7 "$(passes o)"; is "  warned" 0 "$(warned o)"
  m o k7.dvi $a; is "$tool DVI k7 passes" 7 "$(passes o)"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
done
@

\subsection{When latexmk(1) carries on, or starts over}

Force mode is the case where latexmk(1) says it ran out of runs although the
document is broken, which the finishing run has to expose.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== force mode: an error it carried on past is still an error"
FORCE="PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -use-make -8bit -f -e '\$\$max_repeat=7'"
newcase force; doc u $INF '' '\undefinedmacro{}'
m o u.pdf "$FORCE"; is "-f, broken + unsettled rc" 2 "$RC"
is "  latexmk did run out of runs" 1 "$(grep -c '^Latexmk: Maximum runs' o)"
m o2 u.pdf "$FORCE"; is "  and on the next make" 2 "$RC"
newcase force2; doc u $INF
m o u.pdf "$FORCE"; is "-f, clean + unsettled rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
FX="PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -xelatex -use-make -8bit -f -e '\$\$max_repeat=7'"
newcase force3; doc u $INF '' '\undefinedmacro{}'
m o u.pdf "$FX"; is "-f -xelatex, broken + unsettled rc" 2 "$RC"
@

A finishing run that starts over is harder to come by, so we provoke it: a document
that rewrites an input file on every pass, through [[\write18]], and the [[-g]]
option.
The command is in a file of its own because the [[%]] of [[date]] would comment
out the rest of a line of \TeX{}.
These cases hold the check for missing output, a project that moves its output
after the compilation, and the two remaining known limits.
The last case is why those limits remain, since it's what the obvious way to close
them, searching the log for errors, gets wrong.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== latexmk starts over in the finishing run: no output means failure"
rewriting(){
  echo 'printf "%s\n" "$(date +%N)" > stamp.tex' > gen.sh; echo 0 > stamp.tex
}
LP="latexmk -pdf -shell-escape -use-make -8bit"
LX="latexmk -pdf -xelatex -shell-escape -use-make -8bit"
L3="-e '\$\$max_repeat=3'"
REWRITE='\immediate\write18{sh gen.sh}\input{stamp}'
newcase rw1; doc u $INF '' "$REWRITE x"; rewriting
m o1 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=$LX $L3"
is "-xelatex, rewrites its input, no PDF rc" 2 "$RC"
is "  says why" 1 "$(grep -c '^tex.mk: .*left no output' o1)"
is "  no false warning" 0 "$(warned o1)"
m o2 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=$LX $L3"; is "  and on the next make" 2 "$RC"
newcase rw2; doc u $INF '' "$REWRITE x"; rewriting
m o1 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=$LP $L3"
is "-pdf, rewrites its input, has PDF rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o1)"
is "  the PDF exists" yes "$(exists ltxobj/u.pdf)"
newcase g1; doc u $INF
m o1 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -xelatex -g -use-make -8bit $L3"
is "-xelatex -g, no PDF rc" 2 "$RC"
newcase g2; doc u $INF
m o1 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -g -use-make -8bit $L3"
is "-pdf -g, has PDF rc" 0 "$RC"
newcase mvout; doc slides $INF
printf 'ltxobj/slides-incomplete.pdf: slides.tex\n\t${COMPILE.tex}\n' >> Makefile
printf '\tmv ltxobj/slides.pdf ltxobj/slides-incomplete.pdf\n' >> Makefile
m o1 ltxobj/slides-incomplete.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 \
  "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -use-make -8bit $L3"
is "target named apart from its source rc" 0 "$RC"
newcase rw3; plain s.tex "$REWRITE \\undefinedmacro"; rewriting
m o1 s.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=$LP $L3"; is "broken, rewrites its input rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 s.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=$LP $L3"
is "  KNOWN LIMIT next make" 0 "$RC"; is "    warned" 1 "$(warned o2)"
newcase fg1; doc u $INF '' '\undefinedmacro{}'
m o1 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -f -g -use-make -8bit $L3"
is "KNOWN LIMIT -f -g, broken rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o1)"

echo "=== a line starting with ! is not an error"
newcase bang; doc u $INF '' '\typeout{! not an error}'
m o1 u.pdf TEX_MAX_PASSES=3 "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -use-make -8bit $L3"
is "unsettled, ! at start of a log line rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o1)"
@

\subsection{The shell and the terminal}

A project can run its recipes in a stricter shell, and with [[pipefail]] the
pipeline around \TeX{} fails where it otherwise wouldn't.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== strict shells"
STRICT=(SHELL=/bin/bash ".SHELLFLAGS=-eu -o pipefail -c")
newcase st1; doc u $INF
m o u.pdf "${STRICT[@]}"; is "pipefail -e, unsettled rc" 0 "$RC"; is "  warned" 1 "$(warned o)"
newcase st2; doc u $INF
m o u.pdf "${STRICT[@]}" \
  "PDFLATEX=latexmk -pdf -xelatex -use-make -8bit -e '\$\$max_repeat=7'"
is "pipefail -e, unsettled -xelatex rc" 0 "$RC"
is "  the PDF exists" yes "$(exists ltxobj/u.pdf)"
newcase st3; plain d.tex "$BROKEN"
m o1 ltxobj/d.pdf "${STRICT[@]}"; is "pipefail -e, broken run 1 rc" 2 "$RC"
m o2 ltxobj/d.pdf "${STRICT[@]}"; is "pipefail -e, broken run 2 rc" 2 "$RC"
@

Finally, a person at a terminal should see \TeX's output as it happens.
\TeX{} buffers its output when it's going to a pipe, so this takes a real terminal
to test, which [[script]] provides.
The document prints a line and then keeps \TeX{} busy for much longer than we wait
for that line.
<<test cases>>=
echo "=== TeX's output reaches the terminal as it happens (pty)"
live(){ # args for make...: yes if START shows within 10 s
  rm -rf ltxobj d.pdf; rm -f pty.log
  script -qfec "make d.pdf LATEXFLAGS=$NF $*" pty.log </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &
  p=$!; t=0; r=no
  while [ $t -lt 20 ]; do
    grep -aq START pty.log 2>/dev/null && { r=yes; break; }
    sleep 0.5; t=$((t+1))
  done
  wait $p; echo $r
}
newcase live
plain d.tex '\typeout{START}\newcount\m\loop\advance\m by 1 \ifnum\m<100000000 \repeat Good.'
is "latexmk: START visible early" yes "$(live)"
is "pdflatex: START visible early" yes "$(live PDFLATEX=pdflatex)"
@


\section{Targets for merging bibliographies}

To merge bibliographies we will use [[bibtool]] and make's archive rules.
In particular, we extend the general rules we defined in [[portability.mk]].
<<variables>>=
BIBTOOL?=     bibtool
BIBTOOLFLAGS?=--preserve.key.case=on --print.deleted.entries=off -s -d -r biblatex
ARCHIVE.bib?= ${CAT} $(if $(wildcard $@),$@) $% | \
  ${BIBTOOL} ${BIBTOOLFLAGS} -o $@
@


\section{Targets for class and package files}

There are two parts concerning class and package files.
<<targets for class and package files>>=
<<compile sty and cls files>>
<<compile class and package documentation>>
@ These are very similar to what we have done above, especially the 
documentation.

Compiling a class or package from DocTeX source is easier than compiling 
a document.
We can normally create the [[.sty]] and [[.cls]] files by running latex(1) on 
the [[.ins]] file.
<<compile sty and cls files>>=
%.cls %.sty: %.ins
	${LATEX} $<
@

We can then compile the documentation similarly to how we compile normal 
documents.
<<compile class and package documentation>>=
%.pdf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.pdf: %.dtx
	${COMPILE.dtx}
	-${LN} ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@ $@

%.dvi ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.dvi: %.dtx
	<<compile DVI>>
	-${LN} ${TEX_OUTDIR}/$@ $@
@ However, we must tell make(1) how to make a [[.bbl]] etc.\ from [[.dtx]].
<<compile class and package documentation>>=
${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.aux ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.bcf ${TEX_OUTDIR}/%.idx: %.dtx
	${PREPROCESS.dtx}
@


\section{External classes and packages}

Occasionally, we are required to use document classes that are not in 
\ac{CTAN}.
Here we provide targets for some such classes and packages.
<<targets for external classes>>=
<<a general downloader>>
<<Springer LNCS>>
<<biblatex LNCS style>>
<<the RFC bibliography>>
<<PoPETS>>
@

We will now construct a general downloader, then we will use this downloader to
write targets for the external classes we are interested in.
In general, what we want this function to do is to download an archive or 
repository ([[TEX_EXT_SRC]]), extract the files we are interested in 
([[TEX_EXT_FILES]]) to the destination directory ([[TEX_EXT_DIR]]), and 
finally, create symbolic links to those files from the current working 
directory.
<<a general downloader>>=
define download_archive
<<targets for symlinks>>
<<targets for desired files>>
<<targets for archive>>
<<target for cleaning>>
endef
define download_repo
<<targets for symlinks>>
<<targets for desired files>>
<<targets for repo>>
<<target for cleaning>>
endef
@

The first thing we want to do is to generate targets and recipes for how to 
create the symbolic links, assuming that the target files already exists.
First we set up a dependency between the files we are interested in and where 
that file is actually located.
Then we create a recipe which will create a symbolic link between them.
We use the [[notdir]] function~\cite[Sect.\ 8.3]{GNUMake} to remove any 
directory-part since we want to create the symbolic link in the current working
directory.
<<targets for symlinks>>=
$(foreach file,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)},\
  $(eval $(notdir ${file}): ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${file}))
$(notdir ${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}):
	${LN} $$^ $$@
@ Note that we need the [[eval]] command above to evaluate the rule for each 
file, otherwise we would get \emph{one line} with many colons --- which is not 
valid syntax for make(1).
To make it easier to add these files as prerequisites to a target, we also 
provide the following phony target.
<<targets for symlinks>>=
.PHONY: $(1)
$(1): $(notdir ${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)})
@

Now we need something to trigger the download of the archive or the repository.
One way to do this is to add a prerequisite for the files from the archive or 
repository.
<<targets for desired files>>=
$(addprefix ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}): \
  ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}
@ Now we turn to the recipe.
In the case of an archive, we must extract the desired files.
We let the variable [[TEX_EXT_EXTRACT]] contain the extraction command.
<<targets for archive>>=
$(addprefix ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}):
	${TEX_EXT_EXTRACT-$(1)}
@ For a repository we can simply copy the file or create a link.
We prefer the latter.
<<targets for repo>>=
$(addprefix ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/,${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}):
	${LN} ${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}/$${@:${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/%=%} $$@
@

The file [[TEX_EXT_SRC]] can be either an archive or a repository.
We let [[TEX_EXT_URL]] be the \ac{URL} to fetch it from in both cases.
In the case of an archive we do the following.
<<targets for archive>>=
${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}:
	${MKDIR} ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}
	${CURL} -o $$@ ${TEX_EXT_URL-$(1)}
@ In the case of a repository, we simply clone it.
<<targets for repo>>=
${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}/${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)}:
	git clone ${TEX_EXT_URL-$(1)} $$@
@ We note that the directory of the repo should be an order-only 
prerequisite~\cite[see][Sect.\ 4.3]{GNUMake} for the files inside.
Unfortunately this is not the case at the moment.

Finally, we must also do some cleaning.
<<target for cleaning>>=
.PHONY: distclean clean-$(1)
distclean: clean-$(1)
clean-$(1):
	${RM} ${TEX_EXT_FILES-$(1)}
	[ "${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}" = "." ] && ${RM} ${TEX_EXT_SRC-$(1)} \
	  || ${RM} -R ${TEX_EXT_DIR-$(1)}
@

\subsection{Springer LNCS}

Springer's \ac{LNCS} series is used for the proceedings of many 
conferences.
The style files are available on Springer's web site, but unfortunately not 
under any permissive license\footnote{%
  It would be most desirable that they were made available in \ac{CTAN} under 
  an open license.
}.
So, we must, each and every one of us, connect to Springer's server and 
download our own copy.
This is what we automate here.

We use the downloader described above.
<<Springer LNCS>>=
TEX_EXT_FILES-lncs?=  llncs.cls sprmindx.sty splncs03.bst aliascnt.sty remreset.sty
TEX_EXT_DIR-lncs?=    lncs
TEX_EXT_SRC-lncs?=    llncs2e.zip
TEX_EXT_URL-lncs?=    https://resource-cms.springernature.com/springer-cms/rest/v1/content/19238648/data/v1
TEX_EXT_EXTRACT-lncs?=${UNZIP} -u $$< -d ${TEX_EXT_DIR-lncs}

$(eval $(call download_archive,lncs))
@

We also want to add backwards compatibility for when we used [[llncs]] instead 
of just [[lncs]].
<<Springer LNCS>>=
.PHONY: llncs
llncs: lncs
@

\subsection{LNCS style for [[biblatex]]}

There is also \iac{LNCS} style for the [[biblatex]] package available on 
GitHub.
Since it is available on GitHub, we recommend adding it as a Git submodule.
I.e.\ run the following command.
\begin{lstlisting}
git submodule add https://github.com/neapel/biblatex-lncs.git
\end{lstlisting}
This will add a directory [[biblatex-lncs]] to the current directory.

If we do not add it as a submodule we can use the downloader above.
<<biblatex LNCS style>>=
TEX_EXT_FILES-biblatex-lncs?= lncs.bbx lncs.cbx lncs.dbx
TEX_EXT_DIR-biblatex-lncs?=   lncs
TEX_EXT_SRC-biblatex-lncs?=   biblatex-lncs
TEX_EXT_URL-biblatex-lncs?=   https://github.com/NorwegianRockCat/biblatex-lncs.git

$(eval $(call download_repo,biblatex-lncs))
@

\subsection{The RFC bibliography}

Occasionally we want to cite \ac{IETF} \acp{RFC}.
Fortunately, Roland Bless of Karlsruher Institute of Technology provides an 
up-to-date bibliography file for all \acp{RFC}, so we will use that one.
This is a single file, so we do not need to use the downloader.
<<the RFC bibliography>>=
rfc.bib:
	<<download rfc.bib>>
	<<change misc to techreport>>

${TEXMF}/tex/latex/rfc.bib:
	mkdir -p ${TEXMF}/tex/latex/
	<<download rfc.bib>>
	<<change misc to techreport>>
@

We will use curl(1) to download a compressed version from Bless' site.
We let curl(1) output the contents to standard out and pipe it to the 
uncompress(1) utility and, finally, redirect the result to the target file.
<<download rfc.bib>>=
${CURL} -o - http://tm.uka.de/~bless/rfc.bib.gz 2>/dev/null \
  | ${UNCOMPRESS.gz} - > $@ ; \
@

According to \ac{IETF}~\cite[Sect.\ 5.2]{IETFCitingRFCs} the \acp{RFC} should 
be cited as the [[techreport]] BibTeX type.
<<change misc to techreport>>=
${SED} -i "s/@misc/@techreport/" $@
@

We also provide a phony target for these two files.
<<the RFC bibliography>>=
.PHONY: rfc
rfc: rfc.bib ${TEXMF}/tex/latex/rfc.bib
@

Finally, we provide a phony cleaning for cleaning.
The target is named [[clean-rfc]] and is added as a prerequisite for 
[[distclean]] --- this way its recipe will not interfere with any cleaning 
recipe written by the user.
<<the RFC bibliography>>=
.PHONY: distclean clean-rfc
distclean: clean-rfc
clean-rfc:
	${RM} rfc.bib
@

\subsection{Proceedings of the Privacy Enhancing Technologies Symposium}

We would also like to be able to use the PoPETS format.
<<PoPETS>>=
TEX_EXT_FILES-popets?=by-nc-nd.pdf sciendo-logo.pdf dgruyter_NEW.sty
TEX_EXT_URL-popets?=https://petsymposium.org/files/popets.zip
TEX_EXT_DIR-popets?=popets
TEX_EXT_SRC-popets?=popets.zip
TEX_EXT_EXTRACT-popets?=${UNZIP} -p $$< popets/$$(notdir $$@) > $$@

$(eval $(call download_archive,popets))
@
